        // Yes! That's the perfect invariant.
        // So we just need to check that after a non-reverting play(), pot == address(c).balance.
        // Since we can't distinguish win/non-win from outside, we just assert: if the call succeeds, then pot == address(c).balance.
        // This holds for every successful play on C, and fails on M when the play succeeds but no win (random != 0).
        // The fuzzer will try many seeds; some will result in non-winning plays on both C and M (random != 0). On C, it passes; on M, it fails because pot=0, balance=10.
        // Also, it still holds for winning plays on C (pot=0, balance=0). On M, winning plays revert, so ok=false, assertion skipped. So we need the fuzzer to find a seed that results in a non-winning play. That's half the time, so fuzzer will hit it.

        ok = false;
        try c.play{value: 10}() {
            ok = true;
        } catch {
            ok = false;
        }
        if (ok) {
            assertEq(c.pot(), address(c).balance, "invariant: pot equals contract balance after non-reverting play");
        }
    }
}

